<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artemis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artemis was the Greek goddess of hunting, the wilderness, wild animals and childbirth, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leto">Leto</a> and twin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, born, according to myth, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a>. She was worshipped throughout the Greek world, and her temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> was counted among the Seven Wonders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified her with Diana, and in the East she was often equated with local goddesses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artemis.jpg" alt="Artemis" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shonagon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diane_de_Versailles_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_AGER_Ma_589.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The newcomers found a native cult of a mother goddess, whom they identified with <b>Artemis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion combined Anatolian and Greek elements: <b>Artemis</b> of Sardis and the mother goddess Kybebe, the later Cybele, were prominent, and the Lydian kings were generous patrons of Greek shrines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus wrote that the Thracians worshipped gods he equated with Ares, Dionysus and <b>Artemis</b>, and that their kings honoured Hermes above all as their ancestor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On the mainland the Mycenaean Greeks recorded offerings to their gods on clay tablets in the Linear B script, deciphered in 1952: among the names are Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, Hermes, <b>Artemis</b> and Dionysus, and a “Mistress of Athana” who may be an early Athena.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Strettweg Cult Wagon from Austria (c. 600 BCE) has been read as showing a deer goddess or a “Great Nature Goddess” like <b>Artemis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Iberian religion was polytheistic and shaped by Greek, Carthaginian and Phoenician practice, and Tanit, Baal, Melqart, <b>Artemis</b>, Demeter and Asclepius were known and worshipped in the region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
